{# ONE editable line row of a sell-side document's line table — the order form's, the quote form's
   and the standalone invoice create form's, which carried three copies of it.

   Three copies of the row a document is BUILT from is how one screen quietly stops posting a
   column: the markup still renders, the cell still looks right, and the field simply never arrives
   at the save. That had already happened twice in these three files by the time they were counted.

   ## It takes parameters. It never asks which document it is

   There is no `{% if document == 'estimate' %}` here and there must never be one: the moment this
   branches on which document it is rendering it is three templates in one file and worse than the
   three it replaced. Everything a document differs by is an argument, and the two things that
   could not be made into a sensible argument — the Actions cell, and the per-document column list
   — are NOT in here: the Actions cell is delegated whole to `actionsTemplate`, and the columns are
   handed in.

   ## The column list

   `columns` is the table's columns IN ORDER, and this row renders one `<td>` per entry, in that
   order, keyed by `key`. The three documents genuinely have different columns — an order has 14, a
   quote 13, a standalone invoice 10 — so the caller is the only thing that can say which. It is
   the same list the header (`sales_line_head.html.twig`), the colgroup and the shared fee row
   (`sales_fee_line_row.html.twig`) are built from, which is the point: a header and a row that
   disagree about columns is the defect one list exists to prevent.

   A column this row does not know the key of renders an EMPTY cell rather than nothing, so a
   mistyped key shows up as a hole in the table instead of a row silently one cell short of its
   header.

   Column keys (internal identifiers; nothing outside these templates depends on them):

     product         the product picker, or — when `nameInProductCell` — the free-text custom name
                     on a blank row instead, or — when `productLocked` — the line's name as plain
                     text
     description     the free-text custom name as its OWN column (the standalone invoice, which
                     shows the picker and the description side by side rather than one or the other)
     location        the stock location select
     sku
     qty             the quantity box, plus every hint that belongs beside it: the resolved base
                     figure, the stock ceiling, and the backorder/restock-ETA pair
     ordered         display only — how much the line being billed was written for. Only a row that
                     draws down another document's line has one
     remaining       display only — how much of that line is still undrawn, with the base figure
                     under it where the line is packaged. Its twin: same caller, same reason
     weight
     unit            U/M — the line's denomination (#601/#644/#659)
     tax_code
     tax_amount      computed, display only
     cost
     original_price  computed, display only
     price
     subtotal        computed, display only
     batch           order-only: SalesOrderLine is the only line entity with a batch
     actions         delegated to `actionsTemplate`

   `batch` is simply a key the quote and the invoice do not pass, which is what an order-only cell
   looks like when the column list is a parameter. The backorder pair inside `qty` is order-only
   the same way — it renders when the row carries a `backordered` figure and no other document
   puts one there.

   ## An include, not a macro

   Same reason sales_add_line_bar.html.twig is one: three callers with three different parameter
   sets, one of which passes no JS hook at all. As a macro this would be a thirty-argument
   positional signature whose call sites read `null, null, null, null` — the estimate form's
   thirteen-argument macro was already at that point. `with_context = false` makes every caller
   state what it wants in full.

   Parameters
   ----------
   columns            required  the table's columns in order — a list of {key, label} (see above).
                                `label` is the cell's data-label when `labelled`.
   index              required  this row's key in the posted `lines[{index}][field]` group, and the
                                no-JS Remove submit's value. LOAD-BEARING: two rows sharing an index
                                overwrite each other, silently.
   line               required  the row's values, as a FLAT hash — never an entity, never a document.
                                Every caller normalises into the same keys, which is what stops this
                                partial from having to know that a quote line calls its product
                                `line.product` and an order row calls it `line.productId`. Keys, all
                                optional: id, productId, name, location, sku, qty, qtyRendered,
                                qtyMin, qtyMax, weight, unit, unitId, units, baseUnitLabel,
                                baseQuantity, ordered, remaining, remainingBase, available,
                                backordered, fulfillmentStatus, restockEta, taxCode, cost,
                                originalPrice, price, priceRendered, subtotal, batch, unitLabel,
                                baseUnitPrice, resolvedLineTotal, stockOverrideReason.
                                PRESENCE IS THE SWITCH for the optional blocks: a row renders its
                                hidden `id` only if `line.id` is defined, its `qty_rendered` only if
                                `line.qtyRendered` is defined, its base-quantity hint only if
                                `line.baseQuantity` is defined, its quantity box's `min` and `max`
                                only if `line.qtyMin`/`line.qtyMax` are defined, the base figure
                                under the remaining cell only if `line.remainingBase` is defined,
                                and its backorder pair only if `line.backordered` is defined. A
                                caller that does not want a block omits the key rather than passing
                                a flag to suppress it.
   products           required  the catalog for the picker, as rows (id, sku, name, weight, unit,
                                taxCode, cost, originalPrice, price)
   locations          required  the stock locations for the location select
   kind               required  'product' or 'blank' — what this ROW is. It decides the U/M cell
                                (a product DECLARES its unit, so a product row never gets a text
                                box — see the U/M cell below), the catalog-fill hooks, and, where
                                `nameInProductCell`, which control the product cell holds.
   lineClass          required  the document's own row class — 'order-line-row',
                                'estimate-line-row', 'invoice-line-row'. Each document's CSS and
                                each suite's assertions key on it.
   actionsTemplate    required  the template rendering the `actions` cell. See "The Actions cell".
   controlClass       optional  the class every input/select in the row carries besides its hooks —
                                'form-control' on the quote and the invoice, '' on the order, which
                                has never used it anywhere in its line table.
   hook               optional  'order' or 'estimate' — what app.js keys its per-row handlers off,
                                as .js-<hook>-qty, .js-<hook>-price and the rest. Pass '' (or omit)
                                for a document with no line-row JavaScript: every hook class is then
                                simply absent, rather than naming a handler that does not exist.

                                THE QUOTE DOES NOT REUSE ORDER'S HOOK NAMES HERE. app.js binds a
                                full parallel set — .js-estimate-qty, .js-estimate-price,
                                .js-estimate-product-select and eleven more — so the quote passes
                                'estimate' and gets its own. (That is the opposite of
                                sales_address_cards.html.twig, where the quote DOES reuse order's
                                names because app.js binds those document-wide and there is no
                                js-estimate-* twin. The two facts are both true and neither is
                                drift; check app.js before assuming either.)
   labelled           optional  true to put data-label on every cell, for the responsive
                                stacked-table CSS that reads it. The quote and the invoice do; the
                                order does not, anywhere in its line table.
   rowClass           optional  an extra class on the <tr> — 'no-js-row' marks the spare rows that
                                only exist without JavaScript.
   productLocked      optional  true where this row bills a line that ALREADY EXISTS on another
                                document — the invoice-from-order screen, whose rows are that
                                order's uninvoiced remainders. The row's IDENTITY is then that
                                line's and is not editable here, so the three cells carrying it —
                                `product`, `sku` and `unit` — render as plain text instead of as
                                controls, and post nothing. One fact, three cells: which product,
                                under which SKU, counted in which denomination, are all settled by
                                the line being drawn down, and a box offering to change any of them
                                would be offering an edit this screen's save does not read. The
                                hidden `lines[N][id]` still renders, because naming that line is
                                the whole of what such a row posts besides its two figures.
                                Defaults to false, so every other caller is untouched.

                                Orthogonal to `kind`, not a third value of it: a locked row is
                                still a product row or a blank one, and the U/M it prints is
                                `line.unitLabel` either way.
   nameInProductCell  optional  true where the product cell holds EITHER the picker (product row)
                                or the free-text name (blank row) — the order and the quote. False
                                where the picker is always shown and the name has its own
                                `description` column — the standalone invoice, whose rows are one
                                kind rather than two because it has no "Add Blank Line" button to
                                choose between them.
   namePlaceholder    optional  placeholder on the free-text name input
   nameHook           optional  an extra class on that input. The quote's copy carries
                                js-estimate-name; nothing binds it, and it is preserved here rather
                                than dropped in a refactor that is meant to change nothing.
   catalogHooks       optional  true to put the catalog-fill hooks (.js-<hook>-sku, -weight, -cost)
                                on a BLANK row too. The order puts them on product rows only — a
                                blank row has no picker to copy a catalog into it; the quote puts
                                them everywhere. Drift, preserved deliberately; see the report.
   productSelectClass optional  the document's own CSS hook on the picker
                                ('order-line-product-select' and its twins), whose searchable-select
                                overrides are tuned to that table's column widths.
   productSearchPath  optional  where the picker fetches the catalog past the inline limit, and the
                                path the <noscript> id box points at
   productsRemote     optional  whether the catalog is past that document's
                                PRODUCT_SELECT_INLINE_LIMIT (#399). Past it the picker holds only
                                what is already on the line, so it is hidden without scripting and
                                the <noscript> id box is the way in.
   showsWarnings      optional  true where the document reports a coerced figure back on the line
                                (SalesDocumentLineWarnings): the qty and price cells then carry the
                                class attribute the red-cell CSS needs. The invoice has no such
                                mechanism and its cells carry no class at all.
   qtyWarning         optional  paint this row's quantity cell red — the save stored a 0 where a
                                negative number was typed
   priceWarning       optional  the same for price, where what was typed could not be read as a
                                number
   qtyStep            optional  the quantity box's step, defaults to '1'
   taxAmount          optional  the computed tax for this line, always money
   taxCodeBlankLabel  optional  the text on the tax select's empty option. The invoice says "From
                                product" because that is what an empty one means on a screen that
                                snapshots the catalog at save; the order and the quote leave it
                                blank.
   costType           optional  'number' (the order and the quote, which validate it) or 'text'
   priceType          optional  'number' or 'text'. THE QUOTE MUST STAY 'text': a BLANK price is
                                legitimate on a quote and means "No pricing" (#254/#255), which a
                                number input with a step cannot express. The invoice is text for a
                                weaker reason — its placeholder names a fallback, not a rule.
   pricePlaceholder   optional  'TBD' on a quote, naming that rule. Empty on an order.
   priceInputMode     optional  'decimal' on the quote's text box, so a phone keyboard offers digits
   hintHooks          optional  true to put .js-<hook>-base-qty and .js-<hook>-resolved-price on the
                                two conversion hints. The order does and two Cests read them by
                                those names; the quote renders the same hints unhooked.
   subtotalFallback   optional  what the subtotal cell says when there is no subtotal — 'TBD' on a
                                quote, which is the same "No pricing" state the price box names. An
                                order always has one.
   showRemove         optional  render the no-JS Remove submit. Handed to `actionsTemplate`.
   available          optional  stock on hand for this line's product in its region, or null where
                                there is nothing meaningful to state (no product, or a region that
                                does not resolve).
   showsStockOverride optional  true where THIS DOCUMENT'S SAVE reads a per-line override reason —
                                the order form and the standalone invoice create screen, which both
                                measure the shortfall on the post and refuse only while it has no
                                reason against it (#326). The quantity cell then ends with
                                `admin/_stock_override_reason.html.twig`, posting
                                `lines[{index}][stock_override_reason]` under this row's own index.
                                Omitted everywhere else, so no screen renders a box whose save would
                                not read it: the quote form measures no stock, and the
                                invoice-from-order screen bills an order's already-reserved lines.
                                The text that comes back in the box on a refused save is
                                `line.stockOverrideReason`.

   ## Quantities and money

   MONEY renders through the app's own filters in every display cell — `App\Service\DisplayNumber`,
   as `|amount` for a money TOTAL (the line's tax and its subtotal: always exactly two places) and
   `|price` for a UNIT price (the original price: two to six, sub-cent kept). One place owns those
   three rules, which is what stopped one stored figure printing three different ways on three
   screens; `MoneyAndQuantityAreFormattedByTheFilterTest` is what stops the next hand-rolled one.

   The `$` stays here in the template, and so does the subtotal's `is not null` guard: the filters
   render null as the EMPTY STRING and never as `0.00`, deliberately, so an unpriced quote line
   keeps saying what it says instead of claiming to be free.

   The one exception is deliberate and is not money in this sense: the resolved BASE UNIT RATE in
   the price hint is the stored six-place figure passed through untouched, because `$10.00` a case
   of twelve is `$0.833333` an each and rounding that to `$0.83` is how the line total stops adding
   up (#601/#644).

   QUANTITIES ARE PASSED THROUGH UNTOUCHED, and that is load-bearing rather than an omission.

   An order and an invoice are SNAPSHOTS: what was stored is stored. The hidden `qty_rendered` and
   `price_rendered` fields beside the visible boxes exist so the next save can tell "this box was
   never touched" from "the admin retyped the same number" — LineDenomination::boxUntouched() is
   that comparison — so the value rendered INTO an input, and the value carried in its hidden twin,
   have to be exactly what they have always been and exactly each other. Reformatting either one
   turns a round trip that should change nothing into a rewrite of a stored figure: 479 eaches shown
   as 39.9167 cases and multiplied back is 479.0004, which the next save refuses as more than
   remains.

   So no filter, no `+ 0`, no `|number_format`, no `|qty`, `|price` or `|amount` on anything inside
   an input, an option value, a hidden field or a data attribute. The display-only quantity hints are left alone for the same
   reason this whole change is: it is a no-op, and conforming quantity DISPLAY to the app's
   "six places, none when whole" rule is a separate change with its own evidence. See the report —
   what these cells render today depends on whether the figure has been round-tripped through SQLite
   or is still the in-memory NUMERIC(14,4) string, which is a real inconsistency and not this
   change's to fix.

   ## The Actions cell

   Not here, on purpose, and this is the one place the governing rule bit. The three documents'
   Actions cells differ in eight independent ways at once — whether the controls sit directly in
   the `<td>` or inside a wrapper div, which class that carries, whether there is a per-row "+",
   whether the scripted Remove is always rendered or only when the row is removable, whether there
   is a no-JS submit twin, whether the glyph is `x` or `✕`, and whether the label says "Delete" or
   "Remove". Eight parameters for one cell of fourteen, every one of them pure drift, is a partial
   that is worse than the three copies it replaced. So the cell is delegated whole: the caller names
   a template and keeps its own markup, beside the JavaScript that binds it.

   `actionsTemplate` is rendered with {index, kind, showRemove, line, column, labelled}. #}
{# ## Repainting a refused save
   When a save is refused the screen re-renders, and what a person typed must come back — owner
   ruling: "if rejected should print back all users inputs plus rejected reason". The caller hands
   the posted `lines` array in as `submitted` and every box below reads the typed value instead of
   the stored one. Nothing else in this row changes, and a caller that passes no `submitted` (every
   ordinary GET) renders exactly as before.

   The posted names and the `line` keys are the same words, snake_case against camelCase, so this
   is one merge rather than a condition per box.

   **The typed bytes are echoed back UNCHANGED, deliberately.** `qty_rendered` and `price_rendered`
   are compared byte for byte by `LineDenomination::boxUntouched()` to decide whether a box was
   edited. Reformat a repainted value — 50.5 coming back as 50.5000 — and that comparison reads an
   untouched box as edited, which lets the next round trip rewrite a stored figure. So no filter,
   no number_format, no recomputation is applied to anything below. #}
{%- if submitted is defined and submitted[index] is defined -%}
    {%- set typed = submitted[index] -%}
    {%- set line = line|default({})|merge({
        id: typed.id|default(line.id|default(null)),
        productId: typed.product_id|default(line.productId|default('')),
        name: typed.name|default(line.name|default('')),
        sku: typed.sku|default(line.sku|default('')),
        location: typed.location|default(line.location|default('')),
        qty: typed.qty|default(line.qty|default('')),
        qtyRendered: typed.qty_rendered|default(line.qtyRendered|default('')),
        price: typed.price|default(line.price|default('')),
        priceRendered: typed.price_rendered|default(line.priceRendered|default('')),
        cost: typed.cost|default(line.cost|default('')),
        weight: typed.weight|default(line.weight|default('')),
        unit: typed.unit|default(line.unit|default('')),
        unitId: typed.unit_id|default(line.unitId|default('')),
        taxCode: typed.tax_code|default(line.taxCode|default('')),
        batch: typed.batch|default(line.batch|default('')),
        restockEta: typed.restock_eta|default(line.restockEta|default('')),
        stockOverrideReason: typed.stock_override_reason|default(line.stockOverrideReason|default('')),
    }) -%}
    {%- if line.id is same as(null) %}{% set line = line|filter((v, k) => k != 'id') %}{% endif -%}
{%- endif -%}
{%- set hookPrefix = (hook|default('')) ? 'js-' ~ hook ~ '-' : '' -%}
{%- set controlClass = controlClass|default('') -%}
{%- set catalogHook = (kind == 'product' or catalogHooks|default(false)) ? hookPrefix : '' -%}
<tr class="{{ lineClass }} {{ rowClass|default('') }}">
{%- for column in columns %}
    {%- set label = labelled|default(false) ? ' data-label="' ~ column.label|default('') ~ '"' : '' %}

    {%- if column.key == 'product' %}
        <td{{ label|raw }}>
            {# The row's identity, so the next save updates THIS line rather than deleting it and
               creating a replacement — which churned the line primary keys and wrote a
               deleted+created pair per line into the audit log on every save (#267). A row with no
               id is a NEW line, which is exactly what the spare no-JS rows and the rows app.js
               builds from the <template>s are. #}
            {%- if line.id is defined %}<input type="hidden" name="lines[{{ index }}][id]" value="{{ line.id }}">{% endif %}
            {%- if productLocked|default(false) %}
                {# Billing a line that already exists: which product is settled by the line the
                   hidden id above names, so this cell states it and offers nothing to change it. #}
                {{ line.name|default('') }}
            {%- elseif nameInProductCell|default(false) and kind != 'product' %}
                {# A blank line has no product, just whatever the admin types. No matching filler on
                   the product branch: rows are grouped under their own index, so a product row
                   simply omits the key and the save reads that as the '' it always meant. #}
                <input type="text"{% if controlClass or nameHook|default('') %} class="{{ [controlClass, nameHook|default('')]|filter(v => v)|join(' ') }}"{% endif %} name="lines[{{ index }}][name]" value="{{ line.name|default('') }}"{% if namePlaceholder|default('') %} placeholder="{{ namePlaceholder }}"{% endif %}>
            {%- else %}
                {# The product field core shares with every other admin screen —
                   templates/admin/_partials/product_field.html.twig — which is where all three
                   tiers of #399 live: the plain select under the inline limit, the js-only seeded
                   select plus data-search-url past it, and the <noscript> id box that is the only
                   way in past the limit with scripting off. This cell had its own copy of all
                   three; the partial's own first line asks callers not to keep one.

                   Everything the sell side needs beyond what the other callers need is an
                   ARGUMENT, never a branch inside the partial:

                    - `optionData` is the eight-attribute payload the line JavaScript fills a row
                      from on change. The other callers name the product and stop, so they take the
                      default `{sku: 'sku'}` and are untouched. A missed attribute here is silent —
                      nothing errors, the row simply stops filling in — which is why
                      SellSideProductFieldCest asserts all eight individually, by name and value,
                      off the rendered page. `tracksBatch` is a key only the ORDER's product rows
                      carry; the partial renders a missing key as the empty string the quote and the
                      invoice have always rendered there.
                    - `optionText` is the bare product name. A sell-side row shows the SKU in its
                      own column one cell along, so repeating it in the dropdown is noise; a
                      purchase or inventory screen has no SKU column and needs both.
                    - `label: false` keeps this select's accessible name where it has always been,
                      off the control. (An aria-label would be an improvement and is not this
                      change's to make: this refactor is display-identical on purpose.)
                    - `selectedId`, because a line row holds `line.productId` and not the entity.
                    - `selectClass` carries the document's own js- hook and CSS class through.
                    - `tracking-mode` (2026-09-14 lot/serial/expiry plan): which of the two boxes
                      below the legacy batch box applies for the picked product — see
                      productTrackingMode(). #}
                {{- include('admin/_partials/product_field.html.twig', {
                    field: 'lines[' ~ index ~ '][product_id]',
                    products: products,
                    remote: productsRemote|default(false),
                    selectedId: line.productId|default(null),
                    searchUrl: productSearchPath|default(null),
                    placeholder: 'Select a product...',
                    label: false,
                    selectClass: [hookPrefix ? hookPrefix ~ 'product-select' : '', productSelectClass|default('')]|filter(v => v)|join(' '),
                    optionData: {
                        'sku': 'sku',
                        'weight': 'weight',
                        'unit': 'unit',
                        'tax-code': 'taxCode',
                        'cost': 'cost',
                        'original-price': 'originalPrice',
                        'price': 'price',
                        'tracks-batch': 'tracksBatch',
                        'tracking-mode': 'trackingMode',
                    },
                    optionText: ['name'],
                }, with_context = false) -}}
            {%- endif %}
        </td>

    {%- elseif column.key == 'description' %}
        <td{{ label|raw }}>
            <input type="text"{% if controlClass %} class="{{ controlClass }}"{% endif %} name="lines[{{ index }}][name]" value="{{ line.name|default('') }}"{% if namePlaceholder|default('') %} placeholder="{{ namePlaceholder }}"{% endif %}>
        </td>

    {%- elseif column.key == 'location' %}
        {# locationAllowsNone (#669, order-only — see the note at order_line_row's include call in
           form.html.twig): true on the order form, where a location genuinely isn't mandatory
           (nothing downstream requires one), so the picker offers a real blank/"None" option
           instead of forcing one. False everywhere else.

           documentFulfillmentRegion is the header default (#full-parity follow-up): a line that did
           not exist before this render — no `line.id` — starts on the document's own fulfillment
           region rather than a blank or a hard-coded guess, the same "header sets it, the box
           overrides it" rule the controllers apply server-side. An EXISTING line's own stored value
           (including a deliberate blank on the order form) is never displaced by it — this is a
           starting point for a NEW row, not a live re-sync of old ones. Falls back to 'Main' only
           when there is no header region to default to at all (documentFulfillmentRegion blank),
           which is the historical behavior every screen had before this parameter existed. #}
        {%- set _isNewRow = (line.id|default('')) == '' -%}
        {%- set _headerDefault = documentFulfillmentRegion|default('') -%}
        {%- set _selectedLocation = line.location|default('') -%}
        {%- if _selectedLocation == '' and _isNewRow -%}
            {%- set _selectedLocation = locationAllowsNone|default(false) ? _headerDefault : (_headerDefault ?: 'Main') -%}
        {%- endif -%}
        <td{{ label|raw }}>
            <select{% if controlClass %} class="{{ controlClass }}"{% endif %} name="lines[{{ index }}][location]">
                {%- if locationAllowsNone|default(false) %}
                    <option value="" {{ _selectedLocation == '' ? 'selected' : '' }}>None</option>
                {%- endif %}
                {%- for location in locations %}
                    <option {{ _selectedLocation == location ? 'selected' : '' }}>{{ location }}</option>
                {%- endfor %}
            </select>
        </td>

    {%- elseif column.key == 'sku' %}
        {# An em dash and not a blank on the locked branch: a cell stating nothing has to be
           distinguishable from a cell that failed to render, which is why the detail table dashes
           an empty SKU too. #}
        <td{{ label|raw }}>{% if productLocked|default(false) %}{{ line.sku|default('') ?: '—' }}{% else %}<input type="text" class="{{ [controlClass, catalogHook ? catalogHook ~ 'sku' : '']|filter(v => v)|join(' ') }}" name="lines[{{ index }}][sku]" value="{{ line.sku|default('') }}">{% endif %}</td>

    {%- elseif column.key == 'qty' %}
        {# No min, on purpose. A quantity of 0 is a real placeholder row, and a NEGATIVE one is not
           refused either: the save stores 0 and SAYS SO, naming the line in the warning banner above
           the table and painting this cell red (SalesDocumentLineWarnings). min="0" asked the browser
           to refuse the one figure the server deliberately accepts-and-explains, putting that
           explanation out of reach on any form whose native validation was on — which is how the
           same typed number behaved two different ways on the order and the quote (#248).

           A row DRAWING DOWN another document's line is the exception, and states its bounds
           through `line.qtyMin`/`line.qtyMax`. There is no accept-and-explain there: the server
           refuses a quantity past what is left to invoice outright, so the box shows the rule that
           is already enforced rather than letting the browser wave it through for the save to turn
           away. UI only. Nothing here is the guard — a post that never saw this screen meets
           exactly the same refusal. #}
        <td{{ label|raw }}{% if showsWarnings|default(false) %} class="{{ qtyWarning|default(false) ? 'line-cell-error' : '' }}"{% endif %}>
            <input type="number" class="{{ [controlClass, hookPrefix ? hookPrefix ~ 'qty' : '', qtyWarning|default(false) ? 'line-input-error' : '']|filter(v => v)|join(' ') }}" name="lines[{{ index }}][qty]" value="{{ line.qty|default('') }}"{% if line.qtyMin is defined %} min="{{ line.qtyMin }}"{% endif %}{% if line.qtyMax is defined %} max="{{ line.qtyMax }}"{% endif %} step="{{ qtyStep|default('1') }}">
            {# What the server put in the box, posted straight back (#644). The next save compares the
               two: a box that came back unchanged means the LINE'S SIZE did not change, so switching
               the U/M selector re-expresses 40 cases as 2 pallets instead of reading the 40 as pallets
               and multiplying the document twenty-fold. See LineDenomination::boxUntouched(). #}
            {%- if line.qtyRendered is defined %}
                <input type="hidden" name="lines[{{ index }}][qty_rendered]" value="{{ line.qtyRendered }}">
            {%- endif %}
            {%- if line.salesOrderLineId|default('') %}
                <input type="hidden" name="lines[{{ index }}][sales_order_line_id]" value="{{ line.salesOrderLineId }}">
            {%- endif %}
            {# The resolved base figure, beside the box that produced it — the whole point of the
               one-selector design is that the conversion happens in front of a person (#601).
               Rendered only on a line denominated in something other than its base unit; a base-unit
               line would just be restating the box. #}
            {%- if line.baseQuantity is defined %}
                <small class="{{ ['field-hint', hintHooks|default(false) and hookPrefix ? hookPrefix ~ 'base-qty' : '']|filter(v => v)|join(' ') }}">= {{ line.baseQuantity }} {{ line.baseUnitLabel|default('') }}</small>
            {%- endif %}
            {# The stock ceiling, stated where the number is typed (#326/#327).

               On an ORDER it is enforced: a non-draft save is refused when a line exceeds it, so
               showing it here is the difference between an admin knowing before they type and finding
               out from a rejected form. On a QUOTE it is information and nothing else — a quote can
               promise more than exists and is never refused for it — but it is the SAME figure from
               the SAME service, because this quantity becomes an order line the moment the quote is
               accepted and that is where it will be enforced.

               Server-rendered, so it is there with JavaScript off, and it already has this document's
               own reservation added back: an order sitting in Pending for 5 units shows those 5 as
               available to itself rather than as spoken for by someone else.

               "available", not "in stock", on both. They were two wordings for one figure, and the
               figure is availability with holds netted out — "in stock" overstates it. #}
            {%- if available is defined and available is not null %}
                <small class="line-stock-hint {{ (line.qty|default(0)) > available ? 'line-stock-hint-over' : '' }}">{{ available|qty }} available</small>
            {%- endif %}
            {# What the last save actually promised beyond stock (#548), and the one part of it an
               admin types. How much of the line is backordered is decided by BackorderSplitResolver
               at save time against the same numbers the save was validated on; this is read off the
               line, never recomputed for display. Only one document has these fields at all —
               SalesOrderLine — so only one document passes them. #}
            {%- if line.backordered is defined and line.backordered > 0 %}
                <small class="line-stock-hint line-stock-hint-backordered">{{ line.backordered|qty }} backordered &mdash; {{ line.fulfillmentStatus|default('') }}</small>
                <label class="line-restock-eta">
                    <span>Restock ETA</span>
                    <input type="date" name="lines[{{ index }}][restock_eta]" value="{{ line.restockEta|default('') }}">
                </label>
            {%- endif %}
            {# Why this line is being sold or billed past what the region can cover (#326).

               LAST in the cell, under the figures it is about. The reason is a property of one LINE
               — the server reads it as `lines[N][stock_override_reason]` and answers a shortfall
               with whichever of that shortfall's own rows carries one — so the only place the box
               can honestly live is the cell that already owns this row's N. Gathered anywhere else
               it would have to be kept in step with the rows by hand, which is the mistake
               StockShortfall::$lineIndexes exists to make impossible on the server side.

               `showsStockOverride` and not a question about which document this is: the box belongs
               on a row whose SAVE reads it, which is the order form and the standalone invoice
               create screen. The quote form measures no stock and is refused for none — a quote's
               shortfall is answered much later, at conversion, by holding the ORDER as a Draft — so
               it passes nothing and no field appears. Nor does the invoice-from-order screen, whose
               rows draw down an order's own reserved lines and are already capped by them. #}
            {# Shown only where the line is actually short, or where we cannot tell — never on a row
               that is provably within stock. `available` is the same figure line-stock-hint above
               already computed the same comparison against (#326 follow-up): a known, sufficient
               quantity needs no reason box cluttering every row that will never need one. Where
               `available` is unknown (the standalone invoice screen computes no live figure at all —
               see this partial's own docblock) the box still shows, because a screen with no signal
               to compare against cannot safely assume "fine" — that is the one case an admin could
               be typing an oversell with no warning and no way to explain it afterwards. #}
            {%- if showsStockOverride|default(false) and (available is not defined or available is null or (line.qty|default(0)) > available) %}
                {{- include('admin/_stock_override_reason.html.twig', {
                    name: 'lines[' ~ index ~ '][stock_override_reason]',
                    id: 'line-' ~ index ~ '-stock-override-reason',
                    value: line.stockOverrideReason|default(''),
                }, with_context = false) -}}
            {%- endif %}
        </td>

    {%- elseif column.key == 'ordered' %}
        {# Display only. What the line being drawn down was written for, in that line's own unit. #}
        <td{{ label|raw }} style="text-align:right;">{{ line.ordered|default('') }}</td>

    {%- elseif column.key == 'invoiced' %}
        {# Display only. Already billed on OTHER invoices against this order line. #}
        <td{{ label|raw }} style="text-align:right;">{{ line.invoiced|default('') }}</td>

    {%- elseif column.key == 'remaining' %}
        {# Display only, and the figure `line.qtyMax` puts on the box one cell along: how much of
           that line is still undrawn. The base figure goes under it on a packaged line for the same
           reason the qty cell's base hint exists — the conversion belongs in front of a person
           (#601) — and is simply omitted where the line is already in base units. #}
        <td{{ label|raw }} style="text-align:right;">
            {{ line.remaining|default('') }}
            {%- if line.remainingBase is defined %}
                <small class="field-hint">= {{ line.remainingBase }} {{ line.baseUnitLabel|default('') }}</small>
            {%- endif %}
        </td>

    {%- elseif column.key == 'weight' %}
        <td{{ label|raw }}><input type="number" class="{{ [controlClass, catalogHook ? catalogHook ~ 'weight' : '']|filter(v => v)|join(' ') }}" name="lines[{{ index }}][weight]" value="{{ line.weight|default('') }}" min="0" step="0.001"></td>

    {%- elseif column.key == 'unit' %}
        {# U/M — ONE selector per line (#601, #644, #659). It is the LINE's unit: it decides what the
           Qty box counts and what denomination the Price box asks for, and it is persisted, printed,
           and carried onto whatever document this one becomes.

           ## A PRODUCT row never offers a text box

           The cell used to render two different FIELDS out of one column: a packaging <select> where
           the product had a ladder, and a free-text `unit` box where it did not — one column, two
           concepts, nothing on screen saying which one you were looking at. The text box was the
           harmful half. LineDenomination::baseLabel() prefers a line's typed string OVER the
           product's declared base unit, so a typed `12P` beat the product's `EA` and got PRINTED on
           the customer document as the unit the base quantity is counted in. It does not corrupt the
           numbers — the base quantity comes off the unit, never off this label — it corrupts what the
           document says those correct numbers ARE. That precedence is right for a blank line and
           wrong for a product line, and applying it uniformly was the defect.

           So the branch is on the ROW KIND, not on whether the product happens to list any units:

            - product -> one <select>, always. The base unit is its first option and the product's
              available units follow it. A product that lists none shows exactly ONE option, its own
              base unit — which is honest, and blank is allowed (#659), not a misconfiguration.
            - blank   -> the free-text box, kept. A custom line describes something ad hoc with no
              product behind it, so nothing declares its unit and free text is the honest answer.

           Since #659 the options are plain unit CODES — `EA (base)`, then `BOX-12`, `PALLET-240`. A
           term carries its own count, which is what makes `BOX-12` and `BOX-24` two terms, so there
           is no pack size to compose beside the name.

           A plain <select>, because this app works with JavaScript off. #}
        <td{{ label|raw }}>
            {%- if productLocked|default(false) %}
                {# The denomination is the drawn-down line's and travels onto the document this row
                   raises, so there is nothing to choose. `unitLabel` is the same term the price box
                   asks in, which is why this cell states it and the price cell does not repeat it. #}
                {{ line.unitLabel|default('') }}
            {%- elseif kind == 'product' %}
                {%- set baseUnitLabel = line.baseUnitLabel|default('') %}
                <select{% if controlClass %} class="{{ controlClass }}"{% endif %} name="lines[{{ index }}][unit_id]" aria-label="Unit of measure">
                    <option value="">{{ baseUnitLabel in ['', '-'] ? "The product's base unit" : baseUnitLabel ~ ' (base)' }}</option>
                    {%- for unit in line.units|default([]) %}
                        <option value="{{ unit.id }}" {{ line.unitId|default('') == unit.id ? 'selected' : '' }}>{{ unit.code }}</option>
                    {%- endfor %}
                </select>
                {# The legacy free-text U/M snapshot still travels with the row so a save cannot null
                   it, and it keeps the catalog-fill hook so the product-select handler goes on
                   writing the catalog's unit into it exactly as before — the difference is that only
                   the catalog can write it now, never a keyboard. #}
                <input type="hidden"{% if hookPrefix %} class="{{ hookPrefix }}unit"{% endif %} name="lines[{{ index }}][unit]" value="{{ line.unit|default('') }}">
            {%- else %}
                {# No filler [unit_id] on this branch. A row with no selector used to have to post an
                   empty one or the parallel line_unit_id[] array slipped a place whenever the two row
                   kinds were mixed. Rows are grouped under their own index now, so a blank row simply
                   omits the key, which every applyLines* already reads as "the product's base unit" —
                   and a blank row has no product to have a unit of. #}
                <input type="text"{% if controlClass %} class="{{ controlClass }}"{% endif %} name="lines[{{ index }}][unit]" value="{{ line.unit|default('') }}" aria-label="Unit of measure">
            {%- endif %}
        </td>

    {%- elseif column.key == 'tax_code' %}
        <td{{ label|raw }}>
            <select class="{{ [controlClass, hookPrefix ? hookPrefix ~ 'tax' : '']|filter(v => v)|join(' ') }}" name="lines[{{ index }}][tax_code]">
                <option value="">{{ taxCodeBlankLabel|default('') }}</option>
                {%- for code in ['E', 'G', 'S'] %}
                    <option value="{{ code }}" {{ line.taxCode|default('') == code ? 'selected' : '' }}>{{ code }}</option>
                {%- endfor %}
            </select>
        </td>

    {%- elseif column.key == 'tax_amount' %}
        <td{{ label|raw }}><span{% if hookPrefix %} class="{{ hookPrefix }}line-tax"{% endif %}>${{ taxAmount|default(0)|amount }}</span></td>

    {%- elseif column.key == 'cost' %}
        <td{{ label|raw }}><input type="{{ costType|default('number') }}" class="{{ [controlClass, catalogHook ? catalogHook ~ 'cost' : '']|filter(v => v)|join(' ') }}" name="lines[{{ index }}][cost]" value="{{ line.cost|default('') }}"{% if costType|default('number') == 'number' %} min="0" step="0.01"{% endif %}></td>

    {%- elseif column.key == 'original_price' %}
        <td{{ label|raw }}><span{% if hookPrefix %} class="{{ hookPrefix }}original"{% endif %}>${{ line.originalPrice|default(0)|price }}</span></td>

    {%- elseif column.key == 'price' %}
        {# No min: a negative price is deliberate and the save stores it as typed, so the field must
           not claim otherwise. A price that could not be READ is the one that is rewritten — saved
           as 0, named in the banner above, and this cell painted red (#253).

           Blank is "No pricing" on a quote (#254/#255) — the state the TBD placeholder names, and
           the reason that document's box is type=text rather than type=number. Anything typed into
           it that cannot be read as a number is still saved as 0 and reported; it used to be filed
           as "No pricing" instead, so a pasted `1,234.56` silently unpriced the line. #}
        <td{{ label|raw }}{% if showsWarnings|default(false) %} class="{{ priceWarning|default(false) ? 'line-cell-error' : '' }}"{% endif %}>
            {# Per the unit the selector names — `$0.500000/EA` reads as `$6.00` per Case (#644). A
               line in base units answers the stored figure untouched. #}
            <input type="{{ priceType|default('number') }}" class="{{ [controlClass, hookPrefix ? hookPrefix ~ 'price' : '', priceWarning|default(false) ? 'line-input-error' : '']|filter(v => v)|join(' ') }}" name="lines[{{ index }}][price]" value="{{ line.price|default('') }}"{% if priceType|default('number') == 'number' %} step="0.01"{% endif %}{% if pricePlaceholder|default('') %} placeholder="{{ pricePlaceholder }}"{% endif %}{% if priceInputMode|default('') %} inputmode="{{ priceInputMode }}"{% endif %}>
            {%- if line.priceRendered is defined %}
                <input type="hidden" name="lines[{{ index }}][price_rendered]" value="{{ line.priceRendered }}">
            {%- endif %}
            {# THE figure this whole design exists to put in front of somebody (#601): the box asks
               per Case, the column stores per EA, and $10.00 a case of 12 is $0.833333 an each and a
               line total of $399.99 rather than $400.00. The admin sees that here, on the page, and
               can accept it or nudge the price — instead of finding it on a document later with
               nothing to explain it.

               The rate is six places on purpose; see the money note at the top of this file.
               Server-rendered, so it is there with scripting off. #}
            {%- if line.baseUnitPrice is defined %}
                <small class="{{ ['field-hint', hintHooks|default(false) and hookPrefix ? hookPrefix ~ 'resolved-price' : '']|filter(v => v)|join(' ') }}">per {{ line.unitLabel|default('') }} &middot; = ${{ line.baseUnitPrice }} / {{ line.baseUnitLabel|default('') }} &middot; line total ${{ line.resolvedLineTotal }}</small>
            {%- endif %}
        </td>

    {%- elseif column.key == 'subtotal' %}
        <td{{ label|raw }}><span{% if hookPrefix %} class="{{ hookPrefix }}subtotal"{% endif %}>{{ line.subtotal is defined and line.subtotal is not null ? '$' ~ line.subtotal|amount : subtotalFallback|default('') }}</span></td>

    {%- elseif column.key == 'batch' %}
        {# Order-only: SalesOrderLine is the only line entity with a batch column, because a lot is
           allocated at fulfilment and neither a quote nor an invoice raised from nothing allocates
           one. A data-model fact, not drift — which is why the other two documents simply leave the
           key out of their column list.

           Batch/lot (or serial) tracking is a per-PRODUCT attribute, not a line-row default (#670):
           a blank row has no product to track anything for, and a product row only offers the
           capture UI when its product's TrackingPolicy tracks lots or serials outbound. app.js
           re-derives this same test from the picked option's data-tracks-batch whenever a row's
           product changes (syncSellDocBatchVisibility() — no fallback there either, by design). #}
        {%- set showBatchUi = kind == 'product' and line.tracksBatch|default(false) %}
        {# The real lot/serial picker (2026-09-14 lot/serial/expiry plan), alongside the legacy
           free-text batch box above rather than replacing it — that column stays untouched, exactly
           as the plan's "explicitly out of scope" section says. `line.trackingMode` is 'lot',
           'serial', or '' — which of the two boxes below applies, mirroring `line.tracksBatch`'s
           own per-product derivation exactly (see productTrackingMode()). Both boxes start hidden
           and app.js's syncSellDocLotPicker() reveals the right one — the same pattern
           syncSellDocBatchVisibility() already uses for the box above. #}
        {%- set trackingMode = line.trackingMode|default('') %}
        <td{{ label|raw }} class="order-batch-cell">
            <input type="hidden" name="lines[{{ index }}][batch]" class="js-batch-combined" value="{{ line.batch|default('') }}">
            <div class="js-batch-rows"{% if not showBatchUi %} hidden{% endif %}></div>
            <select name="lines[{{ index }}][lot_id]" class="js-lot-select" data-selected="{{ line.lotId|default('') }}"{% if trackingMode != 'lot' %} hidden{% endif %}>
                <option value="">Select a lot&hellip;</option>
            </select>
            <input type="text" name="lines[{{ index }}][serial]" class="js-serial-input" placeholder="Serial" value="{{ line.serial|default('') }}"{% if trackingMode != 'serial' %} hidden{% endif %}>
        </td>

    {%- elseif column.key == 'actions' %}
        {{- include(actionsTemplate, {
            index: index,
            kind: kind,
            showRemove: showRemove|default(false),
            line: line,
            column: column,
            labelled: labelled|default(false),
        }, with_context = false) }}

    {%- else %}
        {# A key this row does not know. An empty cell rather than nothing, so a mistyped key is a
           hole in the table instead of a row one cell short of its own header. #}
        <td{{ label|raw }}></td>
    {%- endif %}
{%- endfor %}
</tr>
